VERDICT: FAIL
CHECKER: checker-second
FAMILY: adversarial
TASK: P17
ATTEMPT: 1
---
Attacked LEDGER_P16_P18_SPEC.md's P17 criteria (1-5) and the tier-3 process
requirement. Summary: the five written behavioral criteria all held up under
direct adversarial testing, but the task is Tier 3 and the mandatory tier-3
acceptance artifacts do not exist and per the spec's own text cannot be
retroactively created -- that is the basis for FAIL, not a code defect.

Technical criteria (1-5), each attacked directly rather than read off the
commit message:

1. staticcheck ./... at pinned v0.8.0 exits 0 at HEAD -- confirmed
   (`go run honnef.co/go/tools/cmd/staticcheck@v0.8.0 ./...`, exit 0).
   Isolated the "last SA4023" claim by checking out 7fe9b98^ in a scratch
   worktree and running the same pinned staticcheck there: it reports
   exactly one finding, SA4023 at ssh_provider.go:79, nothing else in the
   whole module. So this commit's file was provably the only offender and
   removing it is what cleared the module. Claim holds.

2. "Byte for byte" unchanged behavior -- did not trust the diff read. Wrote
   a throwaway _test.go (never committed, deleted immediately after,
   `git status` confirms clean) that drives NewSSHProvider + Unlock on a
   real encrypted-key fixture using the existing test helpers
   (createTestEncryptedSSHKey), and asserted err.Error() equals the exact
   string byte-for-byte. Ran it against HEAD: pass. Ran the *same* test
   against 7fe9b98^ (parent, pre-fix) in a scratch worktree: pass, identical
   string. Confirmed identical on both sides of the commit, not merely
   "looks the same in the diff." Also checked the shadowed-variable detail:
   the new code introduces a fresh `err := decryptSSHKey(...)` inside the if
   block (shadowing the outer read err) and returns unconditionally instead
   of checking err != nil first -- this only matches old behavior *because*
   decryptSSHKey is unconditionally erroring; verified that by reading
   decryptSSHKey's body, which has no branches, no build tags, and returns
   a hardcoded fmt.Errorf with no conditional path to a nil error. It cannot
   ever succeed as written, so this is safe today, but the new code is more
   fragile than the old code: if decryptSSHKey were ever edited to return
   (data, nil) on success, the old code would have used the plaintext; the
   new code would still return an error (fmt.Errorf wrapping nil produces a
   non-nil error string containing "%!w(<nil>)"), silently breaking
   passphrase support instead of surfacing it. Not a defect against current
   behavior, but worth flagging as a latent trap the commit message doesn't
   mention.

3. Stub and test still present and reachable, not just present-but-dead:
   ran `go test ./internal/services/storage/... -run
   'TestDecryptSSHKey|TestSSHProviderUnlockWithPassphrase' -v` -- both PASS,
   both call decryptSSHKey along a still-live path (direct call, and via
   loadIdentity -> Unlock). Neither is orphaned coverage.

4. No other caller depended on the removed assignment: grepped
   `loadIdentity|keyData` across internal/services/storage/*.go
   (non-test) -- decryptSSHKey and the removed keyData reassignment have
   exactly one call site, in the code the commit touched. No other
   AuthProvider (age_provider.go, yubikey_provider.go) touches this.

5. Suite green: `go test ./internal/services/storage/...` -> ok, 35s.

Where the claim fails: this row is Tier 3 (ledger: `P17  3  tests,second
pending  1  lead  sa4023-dead-decrypt-path-escalated-critical-glob-
unverified`), and internal/services/storage/** is in .swarm/critical.globs.
LEDGER_P16_P18_SPEC.md's own closing section, "What this document does NOT
do," states in plain language: it does not make P17 acceptable at tier 3;
gate.sh check requires .swarm/tier3/P17/report.md with a RESOLUTION line
from two blind arms, and "no such arms exist or can now be created for code
that is already on master." I confirmed .swarm/tier3/P17/ does not exist at
all (only .swarm/manifests/P17.1.files is present for this task; other
tier-3 tasks like P15, R1, R4, R11, R12, S2, A1-A3 all have their own
.swarm/tier3/<task>/ directories with oracles). There is no accept.sh oracle
and no report.md for P17, and per the spec's own admission there cannot be
one after the fact.

That means: even though I could not break the behavioral claim after
sustained adversarial testing (criteria 1-5 genuinely hold), the artifact
this checker's PASS is supposed to feed into -- gate.sh check requiring a
RESOLUTION line at this tier -- cannot exist for this task as currently
scoped. Reporting this as PASS would represent the work as acceptable when
the mechanical gate the constitution requires for tier-3 tasks cannot be
satisfied, and the spec document itself flags this as an unresolved,
user-level decision rather than something a checker verdict should paper
over. Defaulting to FAIL on that unresolved structural gap, per instruction,
rather than silently PASSing criteria that were never the whole test.
